WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.305 --> 00:00:03.387
- When I was approached to think
about the Chinese massacre,

00:00:03.387 --> 00:00:05.210
I was like, I know it happened,

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:08.627
I know it was bad, but that's kind of it.

00:00:09.558 --> 00:00:13.562
(melancholy guitar music)

00:00:13.562 --> 00:00:15.355
- I only learn about this event

00:00:15.355 --> 00:00:16.906
literally less than a year ago.

00:00:16.906 --> 00:00:18.601
- If you don't know the
history of your own people,

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:20.000
you're kind of left without this context.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.584
- I really want to learn about it

00:00:21.584 --> 00:00:22.911
and I'm excited to do so,

00:00:22.911 --> 00:00:24.908
but also a little bit nervous and scared

00:00:24.908 --> 00:00:27.728
as to how my perspectives may change

00:00:27.728 --> 00:00:29.464
based on what I learn today.

00:00:29.464 --> 00:00:31.119
- People forget that there was a struggle

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:33.999
and that there still is a
struggle going on today.

00:00:33.999 --> 00:00:37.499
(melancholy guitar music)

00:00:41.824 --> 00:00:44.869
- We are standing right
on Los Angeles Street.

00:00:44.869 --> 00:00:47.395
This is where the
Chinese massacre started.

00:00:47.395 --> 00:00:49.179
It would have been right there.

00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:51.889
What we have now is this plaque

00:00:51.889 --> 00:00:55.806
which memorializes what
occurred 145 years ago.

00:00:57.207 --> 00:01:00.707
(melancholy guitar music)

00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:12.605
The Chinese have been easily identified.

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:14.422
They were not well liked.

00:01:14.422 --> 00:01:15.600
They were looked upon as being a threat

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.910
to the economy, the jobs.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:22.077
(melancholy guitar music)
(mob yells)

00:01:30.393 --> 00:01:33.951
Right now we're standing
above the Los Angeles Mall.

00:01:33.951 --> 00:01:37.272
This is where the Goller Wagon store was.

00:01:37.272 --> 00:01:39.394
Four of the folks were taken right here.

00:01:39.394 --> 00:01:40.540
And you know those wagons,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.984
it was like a repair shop.

00:01:42.984 --> 00:01:46.136
And so the wagons were
tilted up on one end

00:01:46.136 --> 00:01:49.247
and then that was used as an area to

00:01:49.247 --> 00:01:52.005
drop the rope and lynch the person.

00:01:52.005 --> 00:01:54.089
So it's sort of ironic that
right across the street

00:01:54.089 --> 00:01:56.655
from the Federal Office
Building is the location

00:01:56.655 --> 00:01:59.882
where a number of folks got lynched.

00:01:59.882 --> 00:02:02.465
(glass breaks)

00:02:04.312 --> 00:02:06.645
(gun bangs)

00:02:09.132 --> 00:02:10.476
- I keep thinking about my parents

00:02:10.476 --> 00:02:12.884
and how they immigrated
here in a different time

00:02:12.884 --> 00:02:16.301
but if they had been here 145 years back,

00:02:17.184 --> 00:02:19.588
it just could have been their fate.

00:02:19.588 --> 00:02:21.058
- We literally just saw
on the news recently

00:02:21.058 --> 00:02:21.987
that someone wrote an article

00:02:21.987 --> 00:02:25.572
that they were still being
yelled at to go back to China.

00:02:25.572 --> 00:02:27.617
- The fact that you can kind of just erase

00:02:27.617 --> 00:02:30.868
such an event like that is kind of

00:02:30.868 --> 00:02:31.701
ridiculous.
- [Chris] You can curse.

00:02:31.701 --> 00:02:34.131
- [Jess] It's kind of fucking upsetting

00:02:34.131 --> 00:02:35.876
that you can just forget
that this happened

00:02:35.876 --> 00:02:37.444
and put a federal building on it.

00:02:37.444 --> 00:02:40.944
(melancholy violin music)

00:02:42.821 --> 00:02:46.274
- We are now where the
Tomlinson's corral was.

00:02:46.274 --> 00:02:48.730
The balance of the hangings
occurred right here.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.089
Ironically, we're standing

00:02:50.089 --> 00:02:52.486
at the entry to the Hall of Justice.

00:02:52.486 --> 00:02:54.487
- I didn't know it was that bad.

00:02:54.487 --> 00:02:57.987
(melancholy violin music)

00:03:00.277 --> 00:03:01.389
- I think Chinese Americans

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:03.156
just don't like to talk about that.

00:03:03.156 --> 00:03:04.286
I think we like to pretend that

00:03:04.286 --> 00:03:06.279
like Leo said, everything's okay.

00:03:06.279 --> 00:03:07.260
That we've made it.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.187
We're part of the dream.

00:03:09.187 --> 00:03:10.685
That could easily happen today.

00:03:10.685 --> 00:03:13.013
That does easily happen
today, that same fear,

00:03:13.013 --> 00:03:14.162
that same language, that same

00:03:14.162 --> 00:03:16.374
we all look the same mentality.

00:03:16.374 --> 00:03:18.383
(melancholy guitar music)

00:03:18.383 --> 00:03:20.883
(gong chimes)

00:03:24.092 --> 00:03:25.047
(gong chimes)

00:03:25.047 --> 00:03:26.334
- When they were reading the names,

00:03:26.334 --> 00:03:29.720
every hit of a gong, it
just felt so jarring to me.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.972
I would picture the upright wagon

00:03:31.972 --> 00:03:34.555
and each person literally being lynched.

00:03:34.555 --> 00:03:36.053
- What stood out to me the most,

00:03:36.053 --> 00:03:37.724
when they were reading out the names

00:03:37.724 --> 00:03:38.995
at the candlelight vigil,

00:03:38.995 --> 00:03:41.484
were how familiar the names sounded.

00:03:41.484 --> 00:03:43.379
They sounded like people that could know.

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:44.323
- I didn't know that it was

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:45.875
the largest lynching in U.S. history.

00:03:45.875 --> 00:03:47.965
I can see history repeating itself.

00:03:47.965 --> 00:03:50.600
Like you see people being
killed for who they are today

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.222
and that is just a sign to
me that we're not learning

00:03:53.222 --> 00:03:56.365
as much as we should
be as humans, you know?

00:03:56.365 --> 00:03:58.727
- You still hear, "Oh,
they're taking our jobs."

00:03:58.727 --> 00:04:01.171
So how much has really changed?

00:04:01.171 --> 00:04:03.508
- I feel inspired honestly
just to see the folks

00:04:03.508 --> 00:04:05.362
over at the Chinese American Museum

00:04:05.362 --> 00:04:07.876
and everyone that showed
to the event today

00:04:07.876 --> 00:04:09.489
that clearly cared about history

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:12.796
and cared about preserving
our heritage and our culture

00:04:12.796 --> 00:04:15.306
and never allowing our
communities to forget

00:04:15.306 --> 00:04:17.109
the things that happened to us.

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:19.292
- I feel pride now for how resillent

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:21.709
our people have been and are.

